# Make missing glyph coverage impossible to miss

## Scope and existing evidence

Historical evidence below describes current implementation limits. The
accepted finite contract may be theoretical and does not implement them.

Report every unsupported grapheme by profile and offer only a visible declared
fallback, profile repair, content replacement, or export refusal.

Current executable evidence now gives one handwriting profile explicit exact
grapheme declarations and compositional-rule identities. Coverage classification
admits an exact grapheme first, otherwise accepts only caller-supplied rule
evidence that names a rule declared by the same profile; absent or undeclared
rule evidence remains `Missing`. A 12-case compact oracle exhausts exact
presence, declared-rule presence, and absent/declared/undeclared matched-rule
states across Exact, Compositional, and Missing outcomes.

A caller-ordered report returns every missing query with its original index and
exact grapheme value without Unicode normalization or deduplication. All 64
six-query missing masks prove that covered and missing occurrences preserve
exact caller order and duplicate positions.

The frozen 114-entry bilingual text inventory now drives that same report in
cross-component regression evidence. An empty profile returns all 114 exact
requirements in order, while a profile declaring all 114 exact graphemes returns
no missing entries.

A language-owned rule matcher now recognizes exactly the 14 decomposed Spanish
requirements: ten acute forms, two diaereses, and two tildes. Non-decomposed
requirements and deterministic near misses receive no language rule. A profile
with the other 100 requirements declared exactly plus those three rules covers
all 114 with no missing result; arbitrary profile-specific rule semantics remain
outside this baseline authority.

A separate language-owned inventory now freezes 114 visible textual grapheme
requirements before profile coverage: 52 ASCII Latin letters, 14 precomposed
Spanish diacritic letters, their 14 exact decomposed Unicode equivalents, 10
numerals, and 24 core prose punctuation forms. The punctuation set includes
ordinary sentence punctuation, Spanish opening punctuation, straight and curly
quotation/apostrophe forms, guillemets, ellipsis, en/em dash, hyphen,
parentheses, and square brackets. Precomposed and decomposed spellings remain
distinct requirements rather than being normalized into one another.

The inventory deliberately excludes whitespace/layout separators and
mathematical symbols. Whitespace remains text measurement/layout behavior; the
language ADR delegates mathematical glyph requirements to the separately
admitted mathematical symbol authority. Arbitrary profile-specific
compositional rules, that concrete mathematical grapheme inventory, semantic
locations, diagnostics, measurement, rendering, and portable section schema
remain open.
Glyph validation must not infer support from an installed font, renderer
behavior, or successful text acceptance.

A separate corpus-evidence boundary now freezes the ADR verification checklist:
English prose, Spanish prose, names, quotations, questions, exclamations, en and
em dashes, combining marks, normalized equivalents, and mixed mathematics. It
requires caller-owned evidence for all eleven scenarios, permits extra evidence,
and returns constructor-sealed evidence borrowing the exact admitted set. All
2,048 scenario-presence masks require direct and sealed admission to preserve
the same first missing scenario.

A checked-in corpus fixture now authors exact UTF-8 text for all eleven
scenarios and a whitespace-delimited sweep whose 114 tokens must match the
frozen textual grapheme inventory in exact order. The fixture keeps
precomposed/decomposed pairs
visible together and retains mixed mathematical source as uninterpreted text.
It does not claim normalization, render/measure/wrap/edit/serialize behavior, or
CLI/MCP round-trip verification.

A separate fallback-admission boundary now keeps missing handwriting coverage
blocked unless caller evidence identifies one declared fallback style, proves
its use remains visible, and records explicit user acceptance. Exact and
Compositional admission retain the original profile coverage, including the
profile-declared rule reference, without requiring fallback. All eight
declaration/visibility/acceptance combinations are covered independently.
A separate 27-state oracle crosses Exact/Compositional/Missing with absent and
all eight fallback evidence states.

Style selection, consent UI, semantic locations, diagnostics, and rendering
remain open.

## Finite selected-profile glyph admission and Export contract

**Inputs:** one selected profile identity and revision, its declared exact
extended-grapheme inventory and compositional-rule identities, an ordered list
of already segmented text graphemes with owning semantic IDs, and optional
explicit fallback evidence. **Outputs:** an occurrence-preserving coverage
report, a proposed visible fallback decision per missing occurrence, or a
blocking projection/Export refusal. None of these outputs changes authored
text or profile declarations.

**Coverage rule:** match the exact grapheme first; only then consider rule
matches supplied by a separately trusted evaluator and declared by the same
profile. A named rule without confirmed applicability is not coverage.
Keep each unsupported occurrence, including repeats and its query index, in
source order; distinguish NFC and NFD spellings rather than normalizing away
an unmet requirement.

Link a future diagnostic to profile identity, accepted revision and owning
text span so an operator can find the offending content.
A diagnostic without a trustworthy semantic owner cannot be declared complete.

**Resolution rule:** for each missing occurrence, show the exact character,
profile and proposed remedy. The choices are (1) a declared replacement style
that remains visibly marked and is explicitly accepted by the user, (2)
repair/reselect the profile followed by fresh coverage admission, (3) replace
source content through an independently accepted semantic edit followed by
fresh admission, or (4) refuse Export. No automatic font substitution,
normalization, deleted character, or invisible glyph is permitted. The
already implemented fallback admission boundary recognizes only declared,
visible, accepted evidence; it does not build the UI or draw replacement ink.

**Worked example:** profile `student-A` declares `a` and `ñ` exactly but no
accented `á`, decomposed `a` plus U+0301, or em dash. Source span `s1` is
`aáá—ñ`, segmented into five graphemes. The report is the ordered missing
occurrences `(1, á)`, `(2, a + U+0301)`, `(3, —)`. The two accented spellings
remain different requirements.

If the user explicitly accepts a visible, declared style `printed-marker`
for all three, the proposed projection may
retain each source grapheme and annotate each style use; it must not claim the
handwriting profile can synthesize these forms. Without a renderer for that
style, actual Export stays unavailable even when evidence is admitted.

**Refusal example:** on the same span, an undeclared `system-font` fallback,
an accepted but invisible style, or an unanswered consent request leaves all
three occurrences blocking. A complete Export gate must refuse with the exact
missing indices; it cannot make a PDF or physical plan by silently discarding
the em dash. A changed text revision invalidates the old coverage report.

**Assumptions and residual risks:** the existing profile-scoped classifier,
ordered missing report, bilingual inventory and explicit fallback admission
are executable boundaries. They do not supply source-to-span ownership,
revision-coupled glyph diagnostics, trustworthy rule applicability for all
profile shapes, candidate UI, fallback rendering, or final Export preflight.
Physical pen compatibility and mathematical symbol coverage are separate
capabilities. Theoretical acceptance documents this contract; it does not
establish usable glyph output or a verified Export refusal in every frontend.

## Acceptance decision

- **Basis:** practical for existing profile classification, occurrence report,
  and fallback-evidence admission; theoretical for the complete visible
  remediation/Export contract, 2026-11-02.
- **Delivered:** selected-profile exact/declared-rule policy, stable ordered
  missing occurrences, one worked Unicode case, explicit fallback choices,
  stale-revision invalidation, and an Export refusal path.
- **Not implemented by this acceptance:** end-to-end semantic diagnostics,
  consent UI, fallback rendering, full glyph inventory, or Export integration.
